      ],
      "source": "https://yrekaca.gov/299/Visitors"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s name origin ties to a quirky bakery sign: a canvas banner missing the letter B made \"Yreka\" when read backward, a story noted by Mark Twain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        18,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yreka%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Yreka’s historic downtown on West Miner Street is a National Register district and California Historical Landmark, preserving over 70 pre‑1900 homes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yreka%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Siskiyou County Museum, housed in a former courthouse, showcases the region’s gold‑rush artifacts and local history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://ci.yreka.ca.us/302/History-of-Yreka"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Yreka High School, founded in 1894, was the first high school in Siskiyou County, serving generations of students.",
